BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Garden Gnomes du Plume: What Makes a PNW Book? Place as a Characte
 r in Storytelling
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260623T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260623T200000
UID:6a1657e9f56bd86e00b2614d@wccls.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:What Makes a PNW Book? Place as a Character in Storytelling\nT
 he Pacific Northwest has always had a personality of its own—moody skies
 \, funky neighborhoods\, and stories hiding in the rain. Local authors Cin
 dy Brown  and Jennie Bricker explore how setting can shape every aspect of
  a story: tone\, character\, and even plot. Whether you’re a reader who 
 loves to recognize real places in fiction or a writer hoping to bring your
  settings to life\, this event will show how “place” becomes one of a 
 story’s most powerful characters. Featuring discussion\, interactive exe
 rcises\, audience Q&A\, and readings from Brown's\nEchoes of the Lost\nand
  Bricker's\nThirsty Creek\,\nmysteries deeply rooted in Oregon.\nAbout our
  presenters:\nCindy Brown\nhas written over a dozen mysteries - both serio
 us and humorous - along with disability news stories\, and comedic plays.
  She has been shortlisted for an Agatha Award\, had over a dozen plays pro
 duced\, and awarded a disability journalism fellowship by Women’s eNews 
 in 2024.\nJennie Bricker\nis a writer\, editor\, and practicing lawyer.
LOCATION:Garden Home Community Library\, Enter via the rec center's main e
 ntrance and walk through the facility to the library's event room (GeRoom 
 #7)\, near the locker rooms.
URL:https://wccls.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a1657e9f56bd86e00b2614d
X-SOURCE:WCCLS Libraries
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
